WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.521 --> 00:00:02.124
- [Narrator] There was some
kind of chemistry between us

00:00:02.124 --> 00:00:03.518
that we absolutely

00:00:03.518 --> 00:00:05.018
hadn't counted on.

00:00:06.295 --> 00:00:09.910
- Did you display an early
talent for singing, or for--

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:10.743
- I didn't actually.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:11.856
I got thrown out of the choir.

00:00:11.856 --> 00:00:13.226
It's a ridiculous situation

00:00:13.226 --> 00:00:15.727
to sign a contract when
you're 18 years old

00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:16.841
and be held to it

00:00:16.841 --> 00:00:18.452
for your entire professional career.

00:00:18.452 --> 00:00:20.589
I used to get really, really

00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:22.656
paranoid and after every show,

00:00:22.656 --> 00:00:23.828
I'd have to go straight back to the hotel

00:00:23.828 --> 00:00:25.045
and talk to no one.

00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:28.195
- He was an English singer,
songwriter, record producer,

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:31.326
who rose to prominence for
his part in the duo, Wham!.

00:00:31.326 --> 00:00:33.567
He's best known for his
work in the 80's and 90's

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.321
including hit singles,
"Last Christmas," and

00:00:36.321 --> 00:00:38.461
"Wake Me Up Before You Go-Go."

00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:41.567
He sold over 100 million
records worldwide.

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:42.785
He's George Michael

00:00:42.785 --> 00:00:45.095
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:46.686
Rule number two is our personal favorite

00:00:46.686 --> 00:00:48.102
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:48.102 --> 00:00:48.976
you guys like the best.

00:00:48.976 --> 00:00:50.499
And as always, as George is talking,

00:00:50.499 --> 00:00:52.297
If he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:52.297 --> 00:00:54.013
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:54.013 --> 00:00:55.116
and put quotes around it,

00:00:55.116 --> 00:00:57.005
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:57.005 --> 00:00:58.286
Enjoy.

00:00:58.286 --> 00:01:01.175
(whooshing)

00:01:01.175 --> 00:01:05.092
(powerful inspirational music)

00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:12.043
- The first

00:01:13.668 --> 00:01:18.265
mention of George Michael
in my life was by Mark Dean,

00:01:18.265 --> 00:01:20.016
who ran this little label,

00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:23.356
called Innervision, and he said,

00:01:23.356 --> 00:01:26.282
"I've signed an act that
you're going to love

00:01:26.282 --> 00:01:28.199
and they're going to huge,

00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:31.493
and they're called Wham!."

00:01:32.593 --> 00:01:33.426
(Wham! song)

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.571
And I said, "They're called Wham!."

00:01:36.571 --> 00:01:38.360
He said, "Yes, they're called Wham!."

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.439
- [Narrator] They were
starting to get noticed

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:41.094
on the UK music scene,

00:01:41.094 --> 00:01:42.488
but what was it about Wham!,

00:01:42.488 --> 00:01:45.094
that made them stand out
from the competition?

00:01:45.094 --> 00:01:46.452
- I think to be honest with you,

00:01:46.452 --> 00:01:47.452
great songs,

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:49.299
and

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:52.935
a formula that we didn't realize we had.

00:01:52.935 --> 00:01:54.602
These two young boys

00:01:56.424 --> 00:01:58.865
doing this kind of Butch
Cassidy and Sundance thing,

00:01:58.865 --> 00:02:01.184
but in a very, kind of camp way.

00:02:01.184 --> 00:02:03.798
And I think there was
something about the fact

00:02:03.798 --> 00:02:06.716
that it was all very obvious
that our relationship

00:02:06.716 --> 00:02:08.474
as friends was real.

00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:10.140
There was some kind of
chemistry between us

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.874
that we absolutely

00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:13.374
hadn't counted on,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.376
but the world seemed
to see pretty quickly.

00:02:18.376 --> 00:02:22.778
- What about the business
of being what you are now?

00:02:22.778 --> 00:02:25.123
They have the musician, the songwriter...

00:02:25.123 --> 00:02:29.033
Did you display an early
talent for singing or for--

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:29.866
- I didn't actually.

00:02:29.866 --> 00:02:31.089
I got thrown out of the choir.

00:02:31.089 --> 00:02:31.922
- Did you?

00:02:31.922 --> 00:02:32.755
(audience laughs)

00:02:32.755 --> 00:02:34.294
- I was thrown out of the choir for

00:02:34.294 --> 00:02:35.409
I think, as far as I know,

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:36.242
It was for talking.

00:02:36.242 --> 00:02:40.082
And I always remember my
dad used to say to me,

00:02:40.082 --> 00:02:41.569
Ya know, cause I used to say, "Dad...,"

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.107
back when I was like, 16, 17, 18,

00:02:45.107 --> 00:02:47.385
and my Dad would threaten
to throw me out the house

00:02:47.385 --> 00:02:48.636
if I didn't get a job

00:02:48.636 --> 00:02:49.650
within a certain amount of time,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.901
so obviously I went out and got a job,

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:51.832
but I was saying,

00:02:51.832 --> 00:02:53.372
I want to be a singer.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.581
I think I'm going to be rich and famous.

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:59.855
And he would say, "everybody
wants to be a pop singer,

00:02:59.855 --> 00:03:00.731
everybody wants--"

00:03:00.731 --> 00:03:02.771
And I would say, "No, Dad.
Everybody wants to be pop singer

00:03:02.771 --> 00:03:04.653
when they're 12.

00:03:04.653 --> 00:03:05.959
When they're 12.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:07.913
This is my whole life.

00:03:07.913 --> 00:03:09.537
This is what I want to do."

00:03:09.537 --> 00:03:12.363
But, I've lost my train of thought.

00:03:12.363 --> 00:03:14.434
(audience laughing)
- But was it to be

00:03:14.434 --> 00:03:15.405
rich and famous your ambition?

00:03:15.405 --> 00:03:18.562
- No, I think I had two
very, very strong ambitions

00:03:18.562 --> 00:03:20.630
and they've always fought
against one another.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.789
Always.

00:03:21.789 --> 00:03:24.054
And one, was a passion for music

00:03:24.054 --> 00:03:27.721
and an ability to
communicate through music.

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:31.559
And really a sense of
purpose through music.

00:03:31.559 --> 00:03:33.643
And two, was this desire

00:03:33.643 --> 00:03:36.056
to be-- and rich and famous is not right

00:03:36.056 --> 00:03:37.544
because actually rich never came into it.

00:03:37.544 --> 00:03:39.614
I actually realized about six months ago,

00:03:39.614 --> 00:03:41.021
someone put a question to me,

00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:42.775
and I actually realized,

00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:46.234
that at no point during my
early life, when I was...

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:50.067
In all my ambitions, or
even when I started to

00:03:51.246 --> 00:03:53.130
realize my ambitions,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.866
at no point did it ever occur to me

00:03:55.866 --> 00:03:57.672
that one of the byproducts
of this would be

00:03:57.672 --> 00:03:59.505
that I could buy whatever I wanted,

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.954
or live in a big house, have a flashy car,

00:04:01.954 --> 00:04:05.480
all the things that are very pleasant.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.648
But It really hadn't occurred to me.

00:04:06.648 --> 00:04:07.957
It never occurred to me.

00:04:07.957 --> 00:04:08.988
So, I didn't want to be rich,

00:04:08.988 --> 00:04:11.415
I just wanted to be filthily famous.

00:04:11.415 --> 00:04:13.768
I signed that contract

00:04:13.768 --> 00:04:15.685
and I understand the...

00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:19.961
the premise of sanctity of contract,

00:04:19.961 --> 00:04:22.204
but it's a ridiculous,

00:04:22.204 --> 00:04:23.507
such a ridiculous situation.

00:04:23.507 --> 00:04:27.380
Firstly, you are almost
always signing people

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.247
in a very vulnerable position.

00:04:30.247 --> 00:04:32.984
I definitely was in a
very vulnerable position.

00:04:32.984 --> 00:04:34.488
- On the first contract at the age of 18.

00:04:34.488 --> 00:04:35.772
- Exactly. I was at the age of 18.

00:04:35.772 --> 00:04:37.545
And many bands are signed that young.

00:04:37.545 --> 00:04:39.400
Some are signed a little older,

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.660
but whenever they're
signed, they're desperate.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:42.863
Whenever they're signed, they think

00:04:42.863 --> 00:04:45.202
it's going to be their only chance.

00:04:45.202 --> 00:04:46.613
But more importantly

00:04:46.613 --> 00:04:47.753
than the,

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:51.054
the balance of that situation,

00:04:51.054 --> 00:04:52.629
which is very unfair,

00:04:52.629 --> 00:04:54.046
is the fact that,

00:04:55.055 --> 00:04:57.515
there isn't one other industry

00:04:57.515 --> 00:04:59.807
that operates on this basis.

00:04:59.807 --> 00:05:01.274
If I were

00:05:01.274 --> 00:05:02.922
an author,

00:05:02.922 --> 00:05:04.673
and I fell out with my record company

00:05:04.673 --> 00:05:05.961
in the way that I have,

00:05:05.961 --> 00:05:07.676
the worst I would have
to endure was the fact

00:05:07.676 --> 00:05:09.284
that I would have to,

00:05:09.284 --> 00:05:12.585
that they would have the
first option on my next book,

00:05:12.585 --> 00:05:14.834
so to I'm at two books.

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:16.382
It's a ridiculous situation

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.759
to sign a contract when
you're 18 years old,

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:21.850
and be held to it for your
entire professional career.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.112
Why

00:05:23.112 --> 00:05:25.665
why would any court...

00:05:25.665 --> 00:05:28.295
uphold that situation.

00:05:28.295 --> 00:05:30.722
The judge seems to have found, in my case,

00:05:30.722 --> 00:05:32.139
that I reaffirmed

00:05:33.215 --> 00:05:36.504
this contract at the given points

00:05:36.504 --> 00:05:38.640
of my re-negotiations,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.999
but did I really have any choice?

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:43.595
The choice that I had,

00:05:43.595 --> 00:05:46.423
was to go along with that length of term,

00:05:46.423 --> 00:05:47.871
or to do what I've done now,

00:05:47.871 --> 00:05:50.708
which has been an incredibly
difficult process.

00:05:50.708 --> 00:05:52.456
- The abiding fact,
obviously, to the judge

00:05:52.456 --> 00:05:55.296
was that you had voluntarily,

00:05:55.296 --> 00:05:58.868
and with expert advice,
signed these contracts.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:05:59.897
- Are you talking about

00:05:59.897 --> 00:06:01.829
the first contract,
- When all is through,

00:06:01.829 --> 00:06:04.766
he really seems to think--
- All through, yes, I mean...

00:06:04.766 --> 00:06:07.302
- On the one hand, we see your quandary,

00:06:07.302 --> 00:06:09.369
but, for him the abiding fact was,

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.490
and I guess for many people,

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.237
was that you did sign them.

00:06:13.237 --> 00:06:16.122
On balance, you decided it's
better to sign them than not.

00:06:16.122 --> 00:06:19.210
And that's why you're left
with the consequences.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.587
- Oh I think it, but I think
that's a very, very odd

00:06:21.587 --> 00:06:23.004
position to take.

00:06:25.888 --> 00:06:26.971
I think that,

00:06:27.821 --> 00:06:31.359
in all honesty, I think that
there is more to consider

00:06:31.359 --> 00:06:33.150
in this case than

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:34.233
my situation.

00:06:35.565 --> 00:06:36.989
From my point of view,

00:06:36.989 --> 00:06:39.851
I really, honestly do not
expect the public to have any

00:06:39.851 --> 00:06:43.587
great sympathy for a
man who's very wealthy.

00:06:43.587 --> 00:06:45.621
I'm in good health, I'm very wealthy and

00:06:45.621 --> 00:06:48.138
I have a lot of public support

00:06:48.138 --> 00:06:49.616
and I completely accept that.

00:06:49.616 --> 00:06:51.224
I'm not looking for public sympathy.

00:06:51.224 --> 00:06:53.307
I just don't believe that

00:06:54.952 --> 00:06:58.015
If you are wealthy that your principles

00:06:58.015 --> 00:07:00.574
are supposed to be null and void.

00:07:00.574 --> 00:07:04.677
That you're not supposed to fight for...

00:07:04.677 --> 00:07:06.328
for your principles.

00:07:06.328 --> 00:07:09.043
All artists accept that,

00:07:09.043 --> 00:07:11.440
have accepted for years and years,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.001
that they get a considerably
small piece of the pie,

00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:16.995
considering that they are the real product

00:07:16.995 --> 00:07:19.756
and people go out and buy what they give.

00:07:19.756 --> 00:07:21.777
But, I have to be honest,

00:07:21.777 --> 00:07:23.678
It's never been a big problem for me.

00:07:23.678 --> 00:07:26.668
Being paid less than other artists

00:07:26.668 --> 00:07:30.584
has always been a problem
for me in terms of pride.

00:07:30.584 --> 00:07:32.237
In terms of the money I was making,

00:07:32.237 --> 00:07:35.121
I'm lucky enough to be one of the people

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:38.851
that sells enough records,
that I don't care.

00:07:38.851 --> 00:07:40.907
I have a very good lifestyle,

00:07:40.907 --> 00:07:42.025
I'm very wealthy.

00:07:42.025 --> 00:07:43.470
I would also have to add,

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.904
I'm nowhere near as wealthy
as the tabloid press

00:07:45.904 --> 00:07:46.874
would let you believe,

00:07:46.874 --> 00:07:50.838
but I'm perfectly happy with
the money I've got and...

00:07:50.838 --> 00:07:52.762
- They say 18 million.

00:07:52.762 --> 00:07:53.638
- They say 18 million,

00:07:53.638 --> 00:07:55.285
let's just call it a fraction.

00:07:55.285 --> 00:07:56.924
(laughing)

00:07:56.924 --> 00:07:57.802
Am I right?

00:07:57.802 --> 00:07:59.380
But it's still plenty.
- Is the fraction

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:00.301
more than a half

00:08:00.301 --> 00:08:01.249
between a half

00:08:01.249 --> 00:08:02.642
and two thirds?
- I'm not telling you that.

00:08:02.642 --> 00:08:04.234
- Alright, okay.
- I wouldn't tell the court

00:08:04.234 --> 00:08:05.820
that, I'm not going to tell you that.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:06.814
But let me put it this way,

00:08:06.814 --> 00:08:07.956
I think I've got something
to complain about

00:08:07.956 --> 00:08:09.757
simply because in principle it's wrong.

00:08:09.757 --> 00:08:12.142
The end result is that I'm
still a very wealthy man,

00:08:12.142 --> 00:08:13.832
so really, I can
understand people thinking,

00:08:13.832 --> 00:08:15.763
he should shut his mouth,

00:08:15.763 --> 00:08:18.595
but the principle is wrong and this case

00:08:18.595 --> 00:08:20.241
has decided,

00:08:20.241 --> 00:08:22.179
it's not over yet,

00:08:22.179 --> 00:08:24.144
I emphasize it's not over yet,

00:08:24.144 --> 00:08:26.729
but this case will decide

00:08:26.729 --> 00:08:27.896
the future of,

00:08:29.666 --> 00:08:32.166
of all artists in my position.

00:08:33.006 --> 00:08:35.240
There were times in the
last five, six years,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.909
that I was very tempted to just say,

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:38.825
"ya know what, forget it.

00:08:38.825 --> 00:08:40.294
"This is too difficult,

00:08:40.294 --> 00:08:43.031
"you're not out with
the album that you need,

00:08:43.031 --> 00:08:46.029
"and you're driving
yourself crazy with it."

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:46.909
And I was,

00:08:46.909 --> 00:08:49.374
and of course on top of that,

00:08:49.374 --> 00:08:52.021
I was getting quite a
kicking from the press. So,

00:08:52.021 --> 00:08:54.895
at that point in my life,

00:08:54.895 --> 00:08:57.198
with so much going on emotionally,

00:08:57.198 --> 00:08:58.031
being kicked about by the media,

00:08:58.031 --> 00:09:00.614
and the press and the industry,

00:09:03.424 --> 00:09:05.464
almost wishing,

00:09:05.464 --> 00:09:09.515
almost wishing that my career was over,

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:12.015
I was almost ready to give up.

00:09:13.524 --> 00:09:14.782
But part of my motivation,

00:09:14.782 --> 00:09:17.661
a very strong part of my
motivation to keep going,

00:09:17.661 --> 00:09:20.717
was that I always knew
how terrible it would look

00:09:20.717 --> 00:09:25.253
to young gay kids that were
worried about coming out.

00:09:25.253 --> 00:09:27.401
Not that they necessarily
see a great parallel with

00:09:27.401 --> 00:09:31.068
someone who was outed
when they were 35, but

00:09:32.667 --> 00:09:34.614
I think it is a very--

00:09:34.614 --> 00:09:36.754
It would be a horrible way

00:09:36.754 --> 00:09:37.957
to leave my career,

00:09:37.957 --> 00:09:39.366
to leave people thinking that my sexuality

00:09:39.366 --> 00:09:41.033
took away my market,

00:09:42.696 --> 00:09:44.859
'cause I just knew that wasn't true.

00:09:44.859 --> 00:09:47.552
And I felt a kind of responsibility,

00:09:47.552 --> 00:09:50.425
not only to myself, but
to young gay people,

00:09:50.425 --> 00:09:54.248
to say, look, because I
was so absolutely sure that

00:09:54.248 --> 00:09:56.125
my market hadn't left me,

00:09:56.125 --> 00:09:58.309
so I felt it really important to say,

00:09:58.309 --> 00:10:00.265
Look, it is not damaging

00:10:00.265 --> 00:10:02.611
if you have ability,

00:10:02.611 --> 00:10:04.111
it is not damaging

00:10:05.135 --> 00:10:08.052
to have the confidence to come out.

00:10:09.271 --> 00:10:10.682
And in certain parts of life

00:10:10.682 --> 00:10:12.230
it really doesn't matter anymore.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.868
In certain careers it really
doesn't matter anymore.

00:10:14.868 --> 00:10:17.489
And I think just the success
of the record this time 'round,

00:10:17.489 --> 00:10:19.172
and I hope it's continued success

00:10:19.172 --> 00:10:21.755
will alleviate that perception.

00:10:23.676 --> 00:10:25.831
I'm looking forward to getting
more and more comfortable it

00:10:25.831 --> 00:10:28.546
and my voice getting stronger and stronger

00:10:28.546 --> 00:10:29.379
as the tour goes on.

00:10:29.379 --> 00:10:32.507
- [Interviewer] What do you
do to prevent it from...

00:10:32.507 --> 00:10:33.413
- Nothing.

00:10:33.413 --> 00:10:34.718
(laughs)

00:10:34.718 --> 00:10:35.777
- You're just lucky.

00:10:35.777 --> 00:10:37.114
- [George] I'm just lucky. Yeah.

00:10:37.114 --> 00:10:40.752
I have lozenges that I suck the whole day

00:10:40.752 --> 00:10:42.227
out of paranoia,

00:10:42.227 --> 00:10:43.625
but, no, really,

00:10:43.625 --> 00:10:45.754
since I learned to relax about it,

00:10:45.754 --> 00:10:48.637
I've found it much easier, actually.

00:10:48.637 --> 00:10:52.156
I used to get really, really paranoid.

00:10:52.156 --> 00:10:53.714
And after every show I'd have to go

00:10:53.714 --> 00:10:55.527
straight back to the
hotel and talk to no one,

00:10:55.527 --> 00:10:56.521
and it's so dull

00:10:56.521 --> 00:10:58.876
and so boring and actually
led to more throat problems

00:10:58.876 --> 00:11:01.328
because of the anxiety,

00:11:01.328 --> 00:11:02.673
but I'm much more relaxed about it now

00:11:02.673 --> 00:11:04.461
and my voice seems to be fine.

00:11:04.461 --> 00:11:06.628
And I feel ready to do it.

00:11:07.623 --> 00:11:09.548
And it also feels very honest

00:11:09.548 --> 00:11:11.298
It feels very honest.

00:11:12.466 --> 00:11:14.217
I would not be ready to
come out and do another

00:11:14.217 --> 00:11:17.117
three years of a greatest hits tour.

00:11:17.117 --> 00:11:18.977
And even though there
are some of my greatest,

00:11:18.977 --> 00:11:22.341
quite a few of my greatest hits in this,

00:11:22.341 --> 00:11:25.447
it's something completely different.

00:11:25.447 --> 00:11:26.941
And that's what I need right now.

00:11:26.941 --> 00:11:28.387
I don't want to, as I said,

00:11:28.387 --> 00:11:30.659
I'm not really interested
in repeating experiences

00:11:30.659 --> 00:11:31.850
again and again.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.574
And I like to challenge myself

00:11:34.574 --> 00:11:36.825
to improve as a vocalist and believe me,

00:11:36.825 --> 00:11:38.853
singing with an orchestra
is one was to do it,

00:11:38.853 --> 00:11:40.018
really, it really is.

00:11:40.018 --> 00:11:42.232
- [Interviewer] So what
challenges do you see lie ahead?

00:11:42.232 --> 00:11:46.011
- Well, the dance album is a challenge.

00:11:46.011 --> 00:11:50.385
I think it will be a
challenge with the media.

00:11:50.385 --> 00:11:53.614
But the challenge of this tour, really,

00:11:53.614 --> 00:11:56.311
is to see what kind of singer
I can be by the end of it.

00:11:56.311 --> 00:11:58.384
By Christmas, I think I'll see

00:11:58.384 --> 00:12:00.752
just what my potential as a singer is

00:12:00.752 --> 00:12:02.721
at this point in time.

00:12:02.721 --> 00:12:06.064
And I hope I'll be a better
singer than I've ever been.

00:12:06.064 --> 00:12:07.813
- You haven't been all that kindly handled

00:12:07.813 --> 00:12:09.495
by the critics, have you?
- The music critics.

00:12:09.495 --> 00:12:10.604
The bigger...

00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:12.616
- Only the public like you.

00:12:12.616 --> 00:12:13.906
It's the usual syndrome.

00:12:13.906 --> 00:12:16.186
But why do you think the
critics are unamicable to you?

00:12:16.186 --> 00:12:17.440
Why do you think they give you so much--

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:18.853
- I think it's exactly that.

00:12:18.853 --> 00:12:20.579
I think it's because

00:12:20.579 --> 00:12:22.734
we do own up to everything
that we do here.

00:12:22.734 --> 00:12:24.909
We're very honest about
how we market ourselves.

00:12:24.909 --> 00:12:27.201
We're probably the only band at the moment

00:12:27.201 --> 00:12:29.897
who are totally unashamed
of being screamed at.

00:12:29.897 --> 00:12:33.214
And I think, it's like, we do everything

00:12:33.214 --> 00:12:34.470
that everyone else does,

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.816
only everyone else is
very subtly camouflaging

00:12:36.816 --> 00:12:38.440
the ways of selling themselves.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:39.591
And we just think, there's no reason

00:12:39.591 --> 00:12:41.397
that you don't have to...

00:12:41.397 --> 00:12:42.772
It's not insulting your public to do it

00:12:42.772 --> 00:12:44.495
and to tell them you're doing it

00:12:44.495 --> 00:12:46.018
because they enjoy it just the same way.

00:12:46.018 --> 00:12:48.502
Marketing is marketing and image is image.

00:12:48.502 --> 00:12:50.131
No one thinks that's really us.

00:12:50.131 --> 00:12:52.725
I think deep down, I don't
think anybody thinks that we're

00:12:52.725 --> 00:12:55.317
what we portray ourselves to
be in our videos or anything,

00:12:55.317 --> 00:12:58.323
but because we make it so
obvious that we're mocking about,

00:12:58.323 --> 00:13:00.834
I think we blow other people's cover,

00:13:00.834 --> 00:13:03.333
and it's irritating to a lot of people.

00:13:03.333 --> 00:13:05.925
- [Man] In another headline grabbing move,

00:13:05.925 --> 00:13:08.418
George announced that
his next album, Patience,

00:13:08.418 --> 00:13:11.139
will be his last record
on sale to the public,

00:13:11.139 --> 00:13:12.538
and future releases

00:13:12.538 --> 00:13:15.841
will be available for free download only.

00:13:15.841 --> 00:13:17.270
- Well actually, what I said was,

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:18.896
It'd be available for free,

00:13:18.896 --> 00:13:22.150
or for charitable donation
if people wanted to.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:26.211
And the actual truth is
nobody donated anything,

00:13:26.211 --> 00:13:29.815
so I've kind of withdrawn
the idea, as it were.

00:13:29.815 --> 00:13:33.279
What I said and what I
still believe is that

00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:36.122
in order to make my life work for me,

00:13:36.122 --> 00:13:39.289
in an industry which is falling apart,

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:43.122
I think the things that I want to say,

00:13:43.122 --> 00:13:46.077
and there's things that
I still have to say,

00:13:46.077 --> 00:13:49.028
I think are out of place
now in the medium of pop.

00:13:49.028 --> 00:13:51.087
So, I think I might have
to look for other avenues

00:13:51.087 --> 00:13:52.587
in which to write.

00:13:54.288 --> 00:13:57.443
Because, I'm not who I
was, even five years ago.

00:13:57.443 --> 00:13:59.660
I've got some brand new ideas now

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.493
and I want them to be of some use.

00:14:03.458 --> 00:14:06.318
I've always believed that my creativity

00:14:06.318 --> 00:14:08.235
is with me for a reason

00:14:11.131 --> 00:14:15.168
and that always wanting to
have a positive effect in life

00:14:15.168 --> 00:14:19.690
with what you create is something
that is hard to give up.

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.727
Even though it'd be lovely
to be self indulgent,

00:14:21.727 --> 00:14:23.516
I do really believe that there's a reason

00:14:23.516 --> 00:14:26.173
that I was given this gift.

00:14:26.173 --> 00:14:29.039
And it doesn't just
have to be in my voice.

00:14:29.039 --> 00:14:29.872
It's about

00:14:31.371 --> 00:14:32.862
things I want to say.

00:14:32.862 --> 00:14:34.672
- [Interviewer] In the
breaks of this tour,

00:14:34.672 --> 00:14:36.604
I understand that you're
recording a new album,

00:14:36.604 --> 00:14:37.437
which is very different
from the Symphonica tour.

00:14:37.437 --> 00:14:39.437
It's modern dance music.

00:14:40.769 --> 00:14:42.158
How do you combine the two?

00:14:42.158 --> 00:14:46.003
- Well, the thing is, I'm
interested in the two.

00:14:46.003 --> 00:14:46.994
You know what I'm saying?

00:14:46.994 --> 00:14:50.602
I've always had, I think
I'm pretty well known for

00:14:50.602 --> 00:14:52.830
having there being a
fairly even balance in

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:56.997
my successes in terms of
balance and uptempo songs,

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:01.967
and so that's kept my
kind of lyric writing,

00:15:03.606 --> 00:15:06.898
the lyric writing element of
what I do going constantly

00:15:06.898 --> 00:15:08.779
'cause I'm working in my mind on that.

00:15:08.779 --> 00:15:12.465
And this is all much more about my voice.

00:15:12.465 --> 00:15:13.910
So, I think,

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:15.502
I think at my age,

00:15:15.502 --> 00:15:17.369
to be able to have those
two things going on

00:15:17.369 --> 00:15:19.070
and to know that they'll both be heard

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:20.859
to a degree is quite remarkable.

00:15:20.859 --> 00:15:23.625
I'm thrilled about it to
have that kind of momentum

00:15:23.625 --> 00:15:25.649
in my own brain

00:15:25.649 --> 00:15:27.164
at this age.

00:15:27.164 --> 00:15:29.316
- I mean, obviously you can't
come on a show like this

00:15:29.316 --> 00:15:31.459
without mention of the
wanking in the toilet thing.

00:15:31.459 --> 00:15:33.723
(audience laughing)

00:15:33.723 --> 00:15:35.482
- Which wanking in the toilet thing?

00:15:35.482 --> 00:15:36.541
Which one?

00:15:36.541 --> 00:15:37.415
- The arresting.

00:15:37.415 --> 00:15:39.167
- Oh, of course.
- The arresting.

00:15:39.167 --> 00:15:42.009
That one. Yeah. The one we've heard about.

00:15:42.009 --> 00:15:42.842
- That one.

00:15:42.842 --> 00:15:43.675
(audience laughing)
- And, and--

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:44.692
- The time I got caught you mean?

00:15:44.692 --> 00:15:46.109
- Yeah. That one.

00:15:46.109 --> 00:15:50.420
(audience laughing)
Possibly the last time.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:52.109
(applause)

00:15:52.109 --> 00:15:53.508
And this, this is the thing.

00:15:53.508 --> 00:15:54.790
This is the thing, that amazingly,

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:55.793
the way you talk about it,

00:15:55.793 --> 00:15:58.028
is your fantastic sound
of unapologetic about it.

00:15:58.028 --> 00:15:59.658
And you didn't go into hiding,

00:15:59.658 --> 00:16:03.265
you didn't sort of go draw the curtains,

00:16:03.265 --> 00:16:04.726
Like, was it the next night,

00:16:04.726 --> 00:16:06.209
you were out in a restaurant in L.A.

00:16:06.209 --> 00:16:07.675
having dinner?
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:07.675 --> 00:16:09.180
Well, what's to apologize for?

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.338
I'm a gay man, at the time
I was like 36 years old.

00:16:13.338 --> 00:16:15.321
If I'm perfectly honest.

00:16:15.321 --> 00:16:17.126
If I'm perfectly, perfectly honest,

00:16:17.126 --> 00:16:18.876
outside of my career,

00:16:19.901 --> 00:16:21.310
I've had a terrible run of luck that

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.606
started about 10 years ago.

00:16:22.606 --> 00:16:25.394
I've watched a partner die,

00:16:25.394 --> 00:16:27.403
I've watched my mother die,

00:16:27.403 --> 00:16:30.469
just after that, I went to see a

00:16:30.469 --> 00:16:33.017
back surgeon who told me

00:16:33.017 --> 00:16:35.267
that if I didn't have
two vertebrae removed

00:16:35.267 --> 00:16:36.917
within the next six months to a year,

00:16:36.917 --> 00:16:38.353
that I could be paralyzed.

00:16:38.353 --> 00:16:41.042
So you just learn to have
a different perspective.

00:16:41.042 --> 00:16:42.604
I don't give a toss if people have

00:16:42.604 --> 00:16:44.045
a real problem with me

00:16:44.045 --> 00:16:44.878
having...

00:16:46.636 --> 00:16:48.040
(applause)

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:48.873
Thank you.

00:16:51.253 --> 00:16:52.129
(applause)

00:16:52.129 --> 00:16:53.313
I have a different perspective on life.

00:16:53.313 --> 00:16:54.262
I've lost a lot of great things,

00:16:54.262 --> 00:16:55.598
but I still have a lot
of incredible things

00:16:55.598 --> 00:16:57.819
and the press doesn't
really enter in to it.

00:16:57.819 --> 00:17:01.617
I don't care if people never
see my face in the next,

00:17:01.617 --> 00:17:03.772
for the rest of this century.

00:17:03.772 --> 00:17:06.003
The body of work is safe now.

00:17:06.003 --> 00:17:07.835
If I get hit by a bus tomorrow,

00:17:07.835 --> 00:17:10.537
people will remember what I've done

00:17:10.537 --> 00:17:11.817
and they'll still enjoy it.

00:17:11.817 --> 00:17:13.564
That's so lucky.

00:17:13.564 --> 00:17:15.390
People make brilliant music all the time

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.651
that doesn't get played for 20 years

00:17:17.651 --> 00:17:19.517
and for some reason, mine just

00:17:19.517 --> 00:17:21.569
sits in that pocket where it does.

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:24.938
And such an incredible thing to

00:17:24.938 --> 00:17:26.742
to feel that you've achieved that.

00:17:26.742 --> 00:17:28.636
It's very possible that pop has had

00:17:28.636 --> 00:17:30.401
the best out of me that it going to get.

00:17:30.401 --> 00:17:34.239
Even though I want to be making
music 'till the day I die,

00:17:34.239 --> 00:17:37.119
it's perfectly possible to say 25 years

00:17:37.119 --> 00:17:40.771
on the charts is pretty good going

00:17:40.771 --> 00:17:43.939
and you can't expect
to be relevant forever.

00:17:43.939 --> 00:17:46.953
But the body of work I've done already,

00:17:46.953 --> 00:17:48.534
five albums in 25 years,

00:17:48.534 --> 00:17:50.838
not exactly prolific,

00:17:50.838 --> 00:17:52.425
but I think

00:17:52.425 --> 00:17:54.695
pretty good in terms of quality.

00:17:54.695 --> 00:17:56.060
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:58.235
I made this video because my video editor,

00:17:58.235 --> 00:18:00.404
Christina, asked me to.

00:18:00.404 --> 00:18:01.807
Thank you for that, Christina.

00:18:01.807 --> 00:18:03.499
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:18:03.499 --> 00:18:04.870
you want me to profile next,

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:06.034
leave it in the comments below

00:18:06.034 --> 00:18:07.021
and I'll see what I can do.

00:18:07.021 --> 00:18:08.603
I'd also love to know, what did George say

00:18:08.603 --> 00:18:10.793
that had the biggest
impact on you and why.

00:18:10.793 --> 00:18:12.169
Please leave it in the comments

00:18:12.169 --> 00:18:13.851
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:18:13.851 --> 00:18:15.479
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Tuan Nguyen.

00:18:15.479 --> 00:18:18.314
Thank you so much for picking
up 10 copies of my book

00:18:18.314 --> 00:18:20.293
and continuing to spread the love

00:18:20.293 --> 00:18:21.936
and gift them to other people.

00:18:21.936 --> 00:18:23.141
I love the pictures.

00:18:23.141 --> 00:18:24.084
Keep them coming, man.

00:18:24.084 --> 00:18:25.394
And I appreciate all the support.

00:18:25.394 --> 00:18:26.478
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:18:26.478 --> 00:18:28.113
I believe in you.

00:18:28.113 --> 00:18:30.218
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:18:30.218 --> 00:18:32.244
and whatever your one word is.

00:18:32.244 --> 00:18:35.430
Much Love. I'll see you soon.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.340
(whooshing)

